--- body ---

## Context

The v1.13.0 re-baseline (ticket 0618) was the third done by hand. `UPSTREAM`,
`make upstream-status` and `bench/upstream_catchup.py` already carry the
mechanical half — has upstream moved, and what moved under the surface we watch.
What is still hand work is everything downstream of the verdict, and three
specific things went wrong or nearly went wrong this time.

**1. The catch-up's QUIET verdict is a claim about everything, made from a look
at one directory.** `WATCHED = ["src/features/search/"]`. Its own test says the
worst failure this script has is to "say 'none of it is yours' about
everything", and guards against a *typo* in that list — not against the list
being too narrow. At v1.13.0 the narrow set missed `src/config.ts`, which is
where `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_MODEL` became the local model's name and where the
weight-precision selector landed: the two facts that made `README.md`'s R7 row
wrong rather than dated. It also missed `src/features/fulltext/passages.ts` and
`src/tools/`. The verdict text has to be earned, not asserted: either the
watched set covers what the standing actually reasons about, or the residue
outside it is reported beside the verdict so a nil is never mistaken for a nil
overall.

**2. `check_progress.check_baseline` cannot see a stale artifact citation.** Its
docstring says "The page describes the reviewed release, and no other", and it
enforces that over version strings matching `vN.N.N`. A baseline-stamped
artifact directory carries no `v`. Demonstrated on this branch: with `UPSTREAM`
moved to v1.13.0, changing the single word `v1.12.0` on the page took the guard
from two findings to **zero**, on a page still citing `bench/results/smoke-1.12.0/`
nine times and `smoke-1.10.0` twice. Worse, two of those citations were already
stale at the *previous* pin and nothing had caught them: the paragraph
describing the smoke instrument pointed at the v1.10.0 run while `UPSTREAM`
said v1.12.0, and `make check` was green.

**3. A re-baseline touches more files than anything enumerates.** `UPSTREAM`
(four keys, one of them the index schema generation), the mirror in
`bench/index_schema.mjs`, the fixture stamp, `README.md`'s rows and bars and
"Measured against" line, `SYNC.md`'s head note, a new `DECISIONS.md` entry, a
new `verification/UPSTREAM-<version>-REREAD.md`, a new smoke artifact
directory, and the tag-versus-tip decision about which SHA to pin. Nothing in
the tree lists that. Both previous re-baselines rediscovered it.

## What this ticket is NOT

Not a new guard for its own sake. `rules/workflow.md` prices a new guard at two
retirements, and neither of the two checks below is new: both are repairs of a
hole in a guard that already claims to cover it. Item 1 changes a report, not a
gate. If the honest answer to item 3 is a printed recipe rather than a check,
that is the right answer — a checklist nobody can forget beats a guard nobody
can satisfy.

## Actions

1. `bench/upstream_catchup.py`: report the delta OUTSIDE the watched set beside
   the verdict, and widen the watched set to the surfaces the standing reasons
   about. Demonstrate red: find a real upstream range where the narrow set is
   empty and something outside it changed, and show the old text saying "none
   of it is yours" about it.
2. `bench/check_progress.py`: extend the BASELINE clause to baseline-stamped
   artifact paths, default-deny like the version strings. Demonstrate red
   against the state this branch was in at its second commit.
3. A `make upstream-rebaseline` that prints what a re-baseline must touch,
   computed rather than typed: the exact `UPSTREAM` block for the current tip
   (SHA, version, date, index schema generation read from the mirror), the tag
   gap if the tip is past a release, and the list of files and artifacts the
   last two re-baselines had to touch.

## Test

Every item above ships with its red seen first. Item 2's red was already
observed, in the middle of ticket 0618's own work and recorded here because it
will not be reproducible once that branch lands. The sequence, in order:

1. `UPSTREAM` bumped to v1.13.0, page untouched. `check_progress` reports two
   findings — the page never names v1.13.0, and the page names v1.12.0.
2. The single word on the "Measured against upstream" line changed to v1.13.0,
   nothing else. `check_progress` reports **zero findings**.
3. `grep -c 'smoke-1.12.0\|smoke-1.10.0\|SMOKE-1.10.0' README.md` on that same
   tree: **nine**.

Zero findings on a page citing nine artifact paths from two superseded
baselines is the state to reproduce, and the repaired clause must fail on it.
Reproduce it by reverting the citation half of the 0618 page edit while keeping
the version line.

## Invariants

- No guard whose all-clear is reachable by failing to look.
- The catch-up must stay cheap enough to run on every session; widening the
  watched set must not turn every upstream release into TOUCHED for reasons
  nobody acts on.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `make upstream-catchup`'s QUIET verdict is earned over the whole delta,
      not asserted from one directory
- [ ] A stale baseline-stamped artifact citation on the standing page fails
      `make check`, demonstrated red before it is trusted
- [ ] The re-baseline recipe is in the Makefile or in the catch-up's own
      output, not in a session's memory
- [ ] Ticket 0620's leg gets its always-reachable source, or this ticket
      records why it did not
